WEBVTT

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.840
Coding alone, you lose time in small ways.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.120
You fix the wrong file, or you skip the tests.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.000
And most AI tools either wait to be asked,

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.440
or rewrite your code behind your back.

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:16.730
Pairing buddy is the person in the second chair.

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:19.470
It sits beside you in Neovim, watches,

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:22.110
and speaks up only when it matters.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.130
It never touches your files,

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:26.010
unless you hand it the wheel.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:29.200
Two models share the work.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.760
The Watcher is small and fast:

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.740
it scores each moment.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:35.260
Is this worth a word?

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:37.160
The Partner is larger:

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.960
it reads your code, and writes the remark.

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:48.350
Here is a webhook service in Go.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.410
A delivery test is red:

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:52.710
four attempts, where it wants five.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:56.990
You suspect the backoff code, and change the shift.

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:03.400
Pair is the Navigator by default:

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:05.620
it watches and points.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:08.960
It sees you editing a file the failure doesn't name,

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.180
so it reads the worker first.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:15.050
Then it speaks, right under your line.

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:16.510
The shift is fine.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:18.830
The worker gives up one attempt early.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:23.700
Alt J jumps to the exact characters it means.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.090
You fix it, and save.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.670
Pair runs your tests on every save,

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.570
with a command you set once.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:37.260
Delivery passes.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:40.900
But a queue test that passed before now fails.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.000
A regression, and you didn't even run the tests.

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:46.610
Pair tells you why,

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.070
and Alt J takes you to its fix.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:53.640
The old code is struck through,

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:56.340
and the fix waits as ghost text.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.120
Alt Y accepts it.

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:00.120
Save, and it's all green.

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:04.950
Or hand over a change.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:09.229
Pair drive makes Pair the Driver, for one change.

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:13.280
You watch it type, with its own cursor.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.800
Each line lands only when it's whole.

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:20.170
It tests the unsaved buffer, and hands back:

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.130
a tick, a short note,

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:24.790
and one undo takes it all away.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.100
Changed your mind?

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:29.120
Press any key while it types,

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:30.700
and the edit is gone.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:36.170
So, three roles.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:38.790
The Navigator watches and points.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:43.230
The Scout searches, and runs commands in its own pane.

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:46.730
The Driver makes one change, then gives the wheel back.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:50.140
Install it with two commands:

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:53.960
npm install, then pair setup.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.960
Add the Neovim plugin, set your two models,

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.580
and run pair doctor.

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:02.250
The keys to remember:

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:07.050
Alt J, Alt Y, Pair drive, and u.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:09.190
Pair stays in the second chair.
